class Solution {

    int Left;                                                      // Tracks maximum level visited so
     
    void ListLeft(Node root,int level,ArrayList<Integer> list){
        if(root == null) return;                                   // Base case: null node, return
        
        if(Left < level){                                          // First node at this level
            list.add(root.data);                                   // Add leftmost node to result
            Left = level;                                          // Update max level visited
        }
        
        ListLeft(root.left,level+1,list);                          // Traverse left first
        ListLeft(root.right,level+1,list);                         // Traverse right second
        
    }
    
    public ArrayList<Integer> leftView(Node root) {
        ArrayList<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();               // Initialize result list      
        Left = 0;                                                  // Reset max level tracker

        ListLeft(root,1,list);                                     // Start traversal from level 1   
            
        return list;                                               // Return left view
    }
}

//////////////////////// DRY RUN /////////////////////////////

Example: root = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, N, N]

      1
     / \
    2   3
   / \
  4   5

ListLeft(1, level=1):
  Left=0 < 1 → add 1, Left=1
  ├─ ListLeft(2, level=2):
  │   Left=1 < 2 → add 2, Left=2
  │   ├─ ListLeft(4, level=3):
  │   │   Left=2 < 3 → add 4, Left=3
  │   └─ ListLeft(5, level=3): skip (Left=3)
  └─ ListLeft(3, level=2): skip (Left=2)

Output: [1, 2, 4]

//////////////////////// QUESTION  ////////////////////////////

Left View of Binary Tree
Given the root of a binary tree, return its left view.
The left view is the set of nodes visible when viewed from the left side.
Note: If the tree is empty, return an empty list.

Examples:

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Input: root = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, N, N]

      1
     / \
    2   3
   / \
  4   5

Output: [1, 2, 4]
Explanation: Leftmost nodes at each level: 1 (level 0), 2 (level 1), 4 (level 2)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Input: root = [1, 2, 3, N, N, 4, N, N, 5, N, N]

      1
     / \
    2   3
       /
      4
       \
        5

Output: [1, 2, 4, 5]
Explanation: Leftmost nodes at each level: 1, 2, 4, 5
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Constraints:
• 0 ≤ number of nodes ≤ 10^5
• 0 ≤ node->data ≤ 10^5
